The Human-Origin Value Hypothesis (HOVH): A Revised Framework for Empirical Investigation

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

The progressive automation of material production raises a question that extends beyond technological acceptance: how does the social and economic value of a product change when human labor ceases to be necessary for its production? This revised version of the Human-Origin Value Hypothesis (HOVH) incorporates recent empirical findings on the handmade effect, algorithm discount, and AI art perception to refine the theoretical framework and specify empirically tractable predictions. The central distinction remains between human intention (I_H) and human physical execution (E_H), but the revised framework incorporates the specific emotional and cognitive mechanisms—perceived love, perceived effort, uniqueness motives, and revelatory empathy—that empirical research has shown to mediate origin effects. We propose a (2×4) factorial design using an identical ceramic cup across symbolic gifting and private utility contexts, with four origin conditions (H, HR, R, U). The revised framework specifies three testable regimes (primacy of intention, embodiment/sacrifice, and technological utilitarianism), incorporates individual-difference moderators (need for uniqueness, technology attitudes, need for affect), and proposes incentive-compatible elicitation procedures. We formalize the human-origin premium and derive falsifiable predictions regarding the interaction between origin and consumption context. The paper concludes with a staged research program for empirical implementation.

---

1. Introduction

Robotic automation is traditionally analyzed through productivity, labor substitution, employment, cost reduction, and technological acceptance. A different question emerges when the productive capacity of machines becomes sufficiently advanced: what happens to the value of a product when almost anyone can obtain the physical object at negligible marginal production cost?

This question is not equivalent to asking whether consumers like robots. A consumer may simultaneously prefer robots for some products and prefer human production for others. Existing empirical research confirms this intuition. Fuchs, Schreier, and Van Osselaer (2015) demonstrated that consumers indicate stronger purchase intentions for handmade over machine-made products when buying gifts for loved ones, but not for more distant recipients. Granulo, Fuchs, and Puntoni (2021) found that preference for human over robotic labor is systematically stronger in symbolic consumption contexts—products with high symbolic value such as jewelry and suits—than in utilitarian contexts such as toolsets and car brakes. Rix, Berger, Hess, and Rzepka (2025) identified an "algorithm discount" whereby consumers assign lower value to digital products created by algorithms than those created by humans, driven in part by beliefs about the love and effort imbued in the product.

These findings establish that origin of production matters, but they do not fully specify the psychological mechanisms or boundary conditions. The HOVH addresses this gap by proposing that perceived origin becomes a source of value independent of physical functionality, and that this effect is context-dependent.

Let a product be represented as:

X=(F,O,C),

where F represents functional properties, O represents perceived origin, and C represents the consumption context. The conventional economic treatment emphasizes F and price. The HOVH proposes that, for some categories,

U(X)\neq U(F,P)

because the perceived origin of the product becomes part of the consumption utility itself.

The relevant empirical question is:

\boxed{ \text{Does the origin of production become a source of value independent of physical functionality?} }

This revised framework incorporates recent empirical advances to specify the mechanisms through which origin operates, the moderators that condition its effects, and the methodological safeguards required for valid inference.

---

2. Theoretical Foundations: The Handmade Effect and Its Mechanisms

2.1 The Handmade Effect: Established Findings

The handmade effect refers to the tendency for consumers to evaluate handmade products more favorably than machine-made equivalents. Fuchs et al. (2015) provided the first systematic demonstration across four studies, showing that consumers indicate stronger purchase intentions for handmade products, are willing to pay a premium for them (approximately 17% more for a bar of soap), and that this effect is driven by perceptions that handmade products symbolically "contain love." A comprehensive review by Qin, Liu, Xie, and Lv (2025) identifies six distinct mediating mechanisms: perceived nature, perceived uniqueness, perceived quality, perceived effort, perceived love, and psychological ownership.

2.2 The Effort Heuristic

The effort heuristic—the tendency to evaluate the quality and monetary value of an object as higher when its production is perceived as involving more effort—provides a complementary mechanism. Kruger, Wirtz, van Boven, and Altermatt (2004) demonstrated that people valued poems, paintings, and medieval armor more highly when they believed these artifacts required more human effort to produce. For example, when participants learned that an artist spent four hours painting one work of art and twenty-six hours painting another, they reported that the more effortful work was of higher quality and would sell for more money.

Buell and Norton (2011) extended this to service contexts, demonstrating the "labor illusion"—when websites signal effort expenditure, people can prefer longer waits to instantaneous results even when outcomes are identical. This mechanism directly underlies the HOVH's proposed pathway from perceived human effort to increased valuation.

2.3 The Algorithm Discount

Recent research has extended the handmade effect to digital products. Rix et al. (2025) conducted a mixed-methods study comprising 41 qualitative interviews and a choice-based conjoint analysis with 421 respondents in the context of digital news offerings. They found that consumers assign lower value to digital products created by algorithms than those created by humans—a phenomenon they term the "algorithm discount." The effect is driven by consumers' beliefs about the love and effort imbued in the product, their curiosity about algorithmically generated products, and specific product characteristics such as the type of news article. This finding is theoretically significant because it demonstrates that the human-origin premium is not dependent on physical materiality; it operates for digital products as well.

2.4 AI Art Perception and the Revelatory Mechanism

Research on AI-generated art provides further mechanistic insight. A study examining psychological barriers in human appraisal of AI-labeled artworks found a persistent evaluative bias against AI-generated artworks, moderated by four key mechanisms: attitudes toward AI, perceived effort, existential threat perceptions, and emotional focus. Notably, this bias diminishes when evaluative criteria shift from subjective artistic considerations to objective scientific parameters.

More recently, research grounded in the communicative function of art has proposed a revelatory mechanism: art reveals the creator's way of looking at subject matter, which prompts an empathic response toward the creator and shapes evaluations of the artwork. Human artists hold an advantage because their art has a higher "revelatory factor," thereby prompting more empathy. The revelatory factor and empathy consistently account for a substantially larger share of the effect than alternative mechanisms such as trust, love, self-transcendence, awe, effort, creativity, and uniqueness. This pathway operates specifically in art contexts, helping explain what is distinctive about psychological reactions to art.

2.5 Uniqueness Motives and Symbolic Consumption

Granulo, Fuchs, and Puntoni (2021) provided the most direct empirical test of the context-dependence predicted by the HOVH. Across five studies, they demonstrated that consumers favor human over robotic labor more in symbolic consumption contexts than in utilitarian ones. In study 1 (N=72), preference for human production was highest for product categories with high symbolic value (e.g., jewelry, suits) and lowest for low-symbolic-value products (e.g., toolsets, car brakes). In study 2A (N=144), consumers preferred human labor more when the symbolic value of a service was high (getting a tattoo) than when it was low (getting a tattoo removed; M=5.03 vs. M=4.03, t(142)=3.65, p<.0001). In study 4A (N=124), consumers chose human production for more symbolic product components (the frame of eyeglasses: 59% vs. 41%) but not for less symbolic components (the lenses: 23% vs. 77%, Z=6.01, p<.0001).

Critically, they demonstrated that this effect is moderated by individual differences in need for uniqueness. The interaction between production mode and symbolic value was non-significant for individuals low in need for uniqueness (F(1,133)=0.57, p=.451) and highly significant for those high in the trait (F(1,123)=8.78, p<.01). This finding suggests that the human-origin premium is not universal but depends on individual difference variables that the original HOVH treated only as covariates.

---

3. The Central Theoretical Distinction: Intention Versus Execution

The most important conceptual contribution of the HOVH is the separation of human intention from human physical execution. Consider three production architectures:

H=(I_H,E_H), \quad HR=(I_H,E_R), \quad R=(I_R,E_R),

where I represents intention (design, creative direction, authorship) and E represents execution (physical labor). These configurations can generate physically identical products:

X_H\approx X_{HR}\approx X_R.

Yet their perceived origins may differ:

O_H\neq O_{HR}\neq O_R.

The critical empirical question is whether human physical execution is necessary for the human-origin premium, or whether perceived human intentionality is sufficient. The existing literature provides suggestive but not definitive evidence. The algorithm discount research demonstrates that consumers devalue products when both intention and execution are attributed to algorithms. However, the HR condition—human intention with robotic execution—has not been directly tested in a controlled experimental setting.

The revelatory mechanism suggests a prediction: if the human-origin premium operates through empathy with the creator's way of looking at the world, then human intention may be sufficient, because the revelatory factor derives from the creator's perspective, not their physical labor. Under this account, we would predict:

U(H)\approx U(HR)>U(R).

However, the effort heuristic suggests an alternative prediction: if the premium operates through perceived effort, then physical human execution may be necessary, because robotic execution eliminates the visible expenditure of human effort. Under this account:

U(H)>U(HR)>U(R).

The HOVH's first empirical contribution is to specify a design that can discriminate between these competing predictions.

---

4. Three Theoretical Regimes

The experiment is designed to discriminate among three possible regimes.

4.1 Regime A — Primacy of Human Intention

The first possibility is:

U(H)\approx U(HR)>U(R).

Here, human physical execution is not necessary to preserve most of the symbolic value. The consumer cares primarily about I_H, the existence of a human creator with a recognizable intention. Under this regime, robotic manufacturing does not fundamentally destroy human-origin value. The robot functions as an advanced instrument.

This regime is theoretically consistent with the revelatory mechanism: if the human-origin premium derives from empathy with the creator's perspective, then physical execution is irrelevant. It is also consistent with the algorithm discount research, which shows that consumers devalue products when intention is attributed to algorithms, regardless of execution.

4.2 Regime B — Embodiment and Sacrifice

A second possibility is:

U(H)>U(HR)>U(R).

Here, human intention alone is insufficient. The physical effort of the creator carries independent symbolic information. The consumer implicitly reasons: time sacrificed \rightarrow effort \rightarrow care \rightarrow value.

This regime is theoretically consistent with the effort heuristic and with the "contained love" mechanism identified by Fuchs et al. (2015). If the premium operates through perceived effort and emotional investment, then replacing the artisan's physical labor with a robot should reduce perceived authenticity even when the design remains entirely human.

4.3 Regime C — Technological Utilitarianism

A third possibility becomes particularly relevant in private utilitarian consumption:

U(R)\geq U(HR)>U(H).

The consumer may prefer robotic production when human contribution is perceived as unnecessary to the desired function. This could occur because automation is associated with precision, consistency, hygiene, reliability, low cost, scalability, and technological sophistication.

This regime is consistent with research on the "negative handmade effect": handmade products are typically challenging to standardize, less efficient to produce, and may have a higher rate of defective items, which could lead to negative consumer perceptions. Excessive marketing by some companies for handmade products may also create a negative brand image.

---

5. The Role of Consumption Context

The HOVH distinguishes between symbolic consumption (C_s) and utilitarian consumption (C_u). The same object may have different utility functions depending on context:

U(X|C_s)\neq U(X|C_u).

A formal utility model can be specified as:

U_i= \alpha F_i+ \beta Q_i+ \gamma A_i+ \delta H_i+ \epsilon I_i+ \zeta S_i- \lambda P_i,

where F is functional properties, Q is perceived quality, A is perceived authenticity, H is perceived human contribution, I is perceived human intention, S is symbolic/social meaning, and P is price. The key innovation is that the coefficients are context-dependent:

\gamma=\gamma(C), \quad \delta=\delta(C), \quad \epsilon=\epsilon(C), \quad \zeta=\zeta(C).

The HOVH predicts that, under appropriate conditions:

\delta(C_s)>\delta(C_u).

The hypothesis is not that human origin always increases value. Rather:

\boxed{ \text{The marginal value of human origin is predicted to depend on the social function of consumption.} }

Define the human-origin premium as:

HOP(C)=U(H|C)-U(R|C).

The central empirical prediction is:

\boxed{ HOP(C_s)>HOP(C_u) }

under conditions in which human origin carries relational meaning. A stronger version predicts:

U(H|C_s)>U(HR|C_s)\geq U(R|C_s),

while for utilitarian consumption:

U(R|C_u)\geq U(HR|C_u)

may become possible.

---

6. Experimental Design

6.1 Factorial Structure

We propose a (2\times4) factorial design. The first factor is consumption context:

C\in\{C_s,C_u\}.

The second factor is perceived origin:

O\in\{H,HR,R,U\}.

The resulting eight experimental conditions are:

Context Origin Production description
Gift (C_s) H Designed and handmade by a ceramic artisan
Gift (C_s) HR Designed by a ceramic artisan and manufactured by a precision robot
Gift (C_s) R Design generated autonomously by an algorithm and manufactured by a robot
Gift (C_s) U No information about production origin
Personal (C_u) H Designed and handmade by a ceramic artisan
Personal (C_u) HR Designed by a ceramic artisan and manufactured by a precision robot
Personal (C_u) R Design generated autonomously by an algorithm and manufactured by a robot
Personal (C_u) U No information about production origin

6.2 Physical Equivalence and Manipulation Checks

The physical object should remain as constant as experimentally possible:

X_H=X_{HR}=X_R=X_U.

The experimental manipulation concerns information about origin, not the physical product. A pre-test should establish that participants do not systematically distinguish the physical products on quality, design attractiveness, or functional adequacy. Without this control, an observed difference in willingness-to-pay could simply reflect perceived product quality.

A critical addition is a manipulation check asking participants to articulate, in their own words, the difference between the HR and R conditions. If participants cannot articulate this distinction, the theoretical mechanism is not being activated, and the study cannot validly test the intention-execution separation.

6.3 Incentive-Compatible Elicitation

The original HOVH correctly identified social desirability as a threat, proposing choice-based conjoint as a remedy. This revised framework proposes a stronger solution: the Becker-DeGroot-Marschak (BDM) procedure. In the BDM mechanism, participants bid for products, and a random mechanism determines whether they actually purchase at their bid price. This transforms hypothetical willingness-to-pay into consequential choices, reducing the gap between stated and revealed preference.

The BDM method is an incentive-compatible procedure used in experimental economics to measure willingness-to-pay. Research comparing value elicitation methods has found that WTP elicited under the BDM method is substantially higher than WTP elicited under the Multiple Price List method, suggesting that the BDM procedure is more demand-revealing.

6.4 Individual-Difference Moderators

The existing empirical literature demonstrates that the human-origin premium is moderated by individual differences. The following moderators should be measured and incorporated into the statistical model:

Need for uniqueness. Granulo et al. (2021) demonstrated that the interaction between production mode and symbolic value was non-significant for individuals low in need for uniqueness and highly significant for those high in the trait. A validated need-for-uniqueness scale should be administered.

Technology attitudes. Participants should be measured on their general attitudes toward AI, robots, automation, and technological innovation. Research on AI art perception has found that individuals with more favorable attitudes towards AI exhibit mitigated bias against AI-generated artworks.

Need for affect. Research on AI art evaluation found that the bias against AI-generated art is amplified for individuals higher in need for affect. This suggests that emotional engagement with the creative process is a relevant individual difference.

Existential threat. Perceived existential threat induced by AI amplifies bias against AI-generated artworks. A measure of existential threat should be included.

6.5 Dependent Variables and Mediators

The principal dependent variables should include:

Purchase intention (PI_i), willingness-to-pay (WTP_i), perceived authenticity (A_i), perceived human effort (E_i), perceived warmth (W_i), perceived intention (I_i), emotional value (EV_i), and social signaling value (SS_i).

A structural model can then test whether the origin manipulation affects purchase valuation directly or through mediators:

O\rightarrow(E,A,W,I)\rightarrow U.

The revised framework incorporates specific mediators derived from the empirical literature: perceived love (the belief that the creator cared about the product and its recipient), perceived effort (the belief that the creator expended significant labor), perceived uniqueness (the belief that the product is distinctive), and revelatory empathy (the belief that the product reveals the creator's perspective).

---

7. Falsifiability and Predicted Patterns

The HOVH must permit outcomes that contradict it. The following table specifies the predicted patterns for each regime:

Regime Prediction for C_s Prediction for C_u Falsification condition
A: Primacy of Intention U(H)\approx U(HR)>U(R) U(R)\geq U(HR)>U(H) U(HR)<U(R) in C_s
B: Embodiment/Sacrifice U(H)>U(HR)>U(R) U(R)\geq U(HR)\geq U(H) U(H)\approx U(HR) in C_s
C: Technological Utilitarianism U(R)>U(H) U(R)>U(H) U(H)>U(R) in C_u

The strongest evidence would be a statistically significant interaction:

\boxed{ (O\times C) }

showing that origin affects valuation differently depending on consumption context. A statistically non-significant interaction would weaken the HOVH's central prediction and suggest that origin effects are either universal or context-independent.

The hypothesis would be further weakened if:

U(H)\approx U(HR)\approx U(R)

across symbolic and utilitarian contexts, suggesting that origin information is not psychologically meaningful. It would be disconfirmed if:

U(R)>U(H)

systematically in symbolic gifting contexts, without compensating effects from price, quality, or technological attitudes.

---

8. Toward a Theory of Anthropological Scarcity

The experimental framework has a broader theoretical implication. Suppose the degree of automation of an economy is represented by:

\theta\in[0,1].

As \theta\rightarrow1, the quantity of mechanically reproducible goods may become increasingly abundant. The conventional scarcity mechanism associated with production capacity therefore weakens for some classes of goods. But human time remains finite:

T_H<\infty.

Human attention, authorship, intentionality, participation, and physical presence remain constrained resources even under extreme automation. This motivates the concept of Anthropological Scarcity, defined as scarcity arising from finite human participation in an environment where material production is increasingly abundant.

The proposition is not that all human-made goods automatically become expensive. Rather:

\text{automation}\uparrow

may increase the relative symbolic scarcity of certain forms of human contribution.

A conservative formalization of the long-run hypothesis is:

\frac{\partial^2 V(C_s)} {\partial H,\partial\theta}>0

under the HOVH, stating that increasing automation may increase the marginal symbolic value of human origin in some contexts. For utilitarian consumption, the competing hypothesis is:

\frac{\partial^2 V(C_u)} {\partial H,\partial\theta}\leq0.

The empirical question is whether increasing automation produces divergent marginal values of human origin across consumption contexts.

However, the relationship between automation prevalence and human-origin valuation is unlikely to be monotonic. There may be a threshold effect: when automation is rare, human-made goods are the default and carry no premium; when automation is ubiquitous, human-made goods may become either highly valued (as scarce symbolic resources) or devalued (as inefficient and outdated). The direction of the effect may depend on cultural framing, marketing narratives, and the perceived authenticity of the human creator.

---

9. Cross-Cultural Predictions

The HOVH acknowledges the need for cross-cultural replication but does not theorize how cultural variation might affect the predicted effects. Existing research suggests significant cross-cultural differences. A study comparing American and Korean teenagers found that US participants had a greater tendency to use handcrafted items and reported significantly longer periods of actual use. Research on the handmade effect notes that consumers in individualistic societies tend to value artisan-made products with creative and unique characteristics more highly, giving them higher valuations compared to consumers in collectivist societies.

A more theoretically grounded approach would derive cultural predictions from established frameworks. The HOVH might predict that the symbolic consumption effect is stronger in cultures high in individualism (where unique personal gifts signal individual relationships) and weaker in cultures high in collectivism (where standardized gifts may signal group membership and equality). Future cross-cultural replication should test whether the origin \times context interaction is moderated by Hofstede's individualism-collectivism dimension.

---

10. The Uncanny Valley of Production

The HR condition—human design, robotic execution—may trigger distinct psychological responses that are not captured by either the H or R conditions. Research on the uncanny valley suggests that entities that are partially but not fully human can elicit particularly negative responses. An AI system that designs like a human but produces like a robot may fall into a conceptual "uncanny valley" of production, where the partial human involvement is more unsettling than either full human or full machine production.

The experiment should include measures of eeriness, discomfort, and authenticity violation to test for this possibility. If the HR condition produces lower evaluations than both H and R, this would suggest that partial human involvement is not a stable middle ground but a psychologically distinct and potentially aversive configuration. Research on AI-generated food images has found that consumers describe AI-generated content as "revolting" and "unappetizing," with the uncanny valley effect making images appear "alien" and "unsettling." Similar dynamics may operate for physically produced goods that carry traces of both human and machine agency.

---

11. Research Program

The first experiment should not be treated as a final test of the entire theory. It is the first stage of a larger research program.

Stage I — Laboratory experiment. Test the C_s\times O and C_u\times O interactions using the ceramic cup paradigm with BDM elicitation. Include manipulation checks for the intention-execution distinction and measures of the four proposed mediators (perceived love, effort, uniqueness, revelatory empathy). Include need for uniqueness, technology attitudes, need for affect, and existential threat as moderators.

Stage II — Qualitative interviews. Ask participants why they value or reject each production mode. The purpose is to identify mechanisms that the quantitative model may miss, particularly in the HR condition where the intention-execution distinction is theoretically central but psychologically unverified.

Stage III — Product-category replication. Replicate the experiment across food, clothing, artwork, furniture, gifts, household products, and technical products. The existing literature demonstrates that the handmade effect varies by product category, and the HOVH should specify which categories are most likely to exhibit the origin \times context interaction.

Stage IV — Cross-cultural replication. Test whether HOVH effects depend on culture, income, age, technology exposure, and social norms. Use Hofstede's dimensions to derive directional predictions.

Stage V — Longitudinal automation scenario. Expose participants to hypothetical levels of automation \theta_1<\theta_2<\theta_3<\theta_4 and measure whether the value assigned to human-origin products changes as automation becomes increasingly normalized.

---

12. Conclusion

The central question of a robotic economy is not simply whether humans will continue buying robot-made products. The deeper question is: when machines can manufacture almost anything, what remains economically valuable about human participation?

The Human-Origin Value Hypothesis proposes one possible answer:

\boxed{ \text{Human participation may become a source of value precisely because it becomes unnecessary for production.} }

This creates a potential paradox. The more automation eliminates the necessity of human labor (\theta\uparrow), the less human labor may be required for the production of a physical object. Yet in some forms of consumption, the fact that a human chose to spend time, attention, intention, and effort creating that object may become increasingly meaningful.

The robot can therefore eliminate the economic necessity of human production without necessarily eliminating the social value of human creation. The decisive empirical question is whether consumers value human intention, human embodiment, or both. The existing empirical literature provides suggestive evidence for both mechanisms: the algorithm discount demonstrates that intention matters, while the effort heuristic demonstrates that embodiment matters. The HR condition—human intention with robotic execution—is the critical test case that can discriminate between these mechanisms.

The proposed H/HR/R/U experiment, with its incorporation of incentive-compatible elicitation, individual-difference moderators, and manipulation checks for the intention-execution distinction, is designed to provide this discrimination. If the results support the hypothesis, the implication is not simply that consumers prefer handmade goods. It is more fundamental:

\boxed{ \text{Automation may transform human contribution from a productive necessity into a scarce symbolic resource.} }

That possibility defines the research program proposed here.


